---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
const tree = `README.md
SKELETON.md
AI_AUTHORING_GUIDE.md
book/
  README.md
  volumes/
    01-foundations/
      README.md
      001-a-place-to-begin.md
      002-the-next-step.md
  images/
    example.png
  _ai/
    CONTINUATION_STATE.md
    chapter-summaries/
      001-a-place-to-begin-summary.md`;
const yaml = `---
book: The Curious Learner's Book
volume: 1
volume_title: Foundations
chapter: 1
title: A place to begin
slug: a-place-to-begin
status: complete
summary: ../../_ai/chapter-summaries/001-a-place-to-begin-summary.md
---

# A place to begin

Your chapter starts here.`;
---
<Layout title="Book format — Chapter" description="Publish a Markdown book that works in Chapter, with ordered chapters, YAML metadata, and local images.">
<header class="site-header"><div class="header-inner"><a class="brand" href={base}><span class="brand-mark">▤</span>chapter<span class="brand-dot">.</span></a><a class="text-button" href={`${base}#library`}>← Back to library</a></div></header>
<main class="page format-page">
<span class="eyebrow">A HOME FOR YOUR WORDS</span><h1>A simple format.<br />A whole world of ideas.</h1><p class="format-lead">Chapter reads public GitHub repositories without login, and private repositories when you save a fine-grained read-only token in Library settings. Keep the structure familiar, the chapters ordered, and the images close to the text.</p>
<section><h2>The book lives in <code>book/</code></h2><p>Create a <code>book/README.md</code> index with the book title as its first heading, a short description, and relative links to your volumes. Add chapter files under <code>book/volumes/</code>. You can choose a different root folder when importing.</p><pre><code>{tree}</code></pre></section>
<section><h2>Give each chapter an identity</h2><p>Begin each chapter with YAML front matter between <code>---</code> lines. Keep its slug stable when updating the book so bookmarks and reading progress can follow the chapter.</p><pre><code>{yaml}</code></pre><div class="table-frame"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>volume</code>, <code>chapter</code></td><td>Positive integers determine reading order. Chapter numbers must be unique within each volume.</td></tr><tr><td><code>volume_title</code></td><td>Use the same title for every chapter in a volume.</td></tr><tr><td><code>title</code></td><td>Shown in contents and search. A first-level Markdown heading can supply it if omitted.</td></tr><tr><td><code>slug</code></td><td>A unique, lasting chapter identity. The file path is used if omitted.</td></tr><tr><td><code>status</code></td><td>Use planned, draft, drafting, in-progress, review, or complete. Incomplete material is allowed and shown as an import warning.</td></tr><tr><td><code>book</code>, <code>summary</code></td><td>Useful authoring context. Reading titles come from the book index; summaries are kept outside reading chapters.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Numbered directory and file names can supply missing volume and chapter numbers. Explicit metadata is clearer. The reader ignores volume indexes, files with names starting with <code>_</code>, and material under <code>book/_ai/</code>.</p></section>
<section><h2>Make it travel well</h2><p>Use relative Markdown links for other chapters and heading anchors. Store images in the repository and reference them with relative paths. Chapters, Markdown indexes, and local images are downloaded from one fixed commit before a book becomes available on your shelf.</p><pre><code>{'[Next chapter](002-the-next-step.md)\n[An explanation](002-the-next-step.md#an-explanation)\n![A useful example](../../images/example.png)'}</code></pre><p>Links to missing chapters or heading anchors are reported as warnings. You can import the available chapters while the book is still being written. Chapters marked draft or drafting are also allowed with warnings.</p><p>Label fenced code blocks with their language for syntax highlighting. Wide tables and code blocks scroll horizontally. External links remain links; external images cannot be available offline. Raw HTML and MDX are not executed.</p></section>
<section><h2>Write a little, keep your place</h2><p>The <a href={`${base}#create`}>authoring toolkit</a> generates a repository scaffold and prompts for outlining, writing, continuing, checking sources, exercises, review, and publication. Use them with the AI tool of your choice.</p><p>Keep the agreed learning journey in <code>SKELETON.md</code>, writing instructions in <code>AI_AUTHORING_GUIDE.md</code>, and handoffs under <code>book/_ai/</code>. Update each chapter summary and <code>CONTINUATION_STATE.md</code> after a writing session: record what was actually written, open questions, verified sources, and the exact next section.</p></section>
<section><h2>Publish, check, read</h2><p>Publish the repository publicly or privately on GitHub. Public books need no token. For private books, create a fine-grained token limited to the repository and Contents read permission, then save it in Library settings. In the authoring toolkit, validate the URL to check metadata, chapter order, internal links, and local images. Add the book to your library; each saved edition records its exact commit.</p><p>Download while online and wait for the app's offline-ready status. Your books, reading position, bookmarks, and preferences stay in this browser. The GitHub token is stored in this browser and is not included in library backups. Export a library backup before clearing browser data or changing devices.</p><a class="button primary" href={`${base}#create`}>Create your book →</a></section>
</main><footer class="site-footer"><span class="footer-brand">chapter.</span><p>A little more curious, one page at a time.</p><a href={base}>My library</a></footer>
</Layout>
